Local Debian 13 build
====================

Base: upstream v1.4.7 and Debian source package 1.4.7-1.
The Debian patch series is retained. The local packaging fixes the
Recommends field name, uses watch format 4 and an explicit Priority for
Trixie tooling, and excludes .deb-build from source diffs.
Debian build flags are exported, including those for Intel GPU C sources.
This is a local backport and is not published in the Debian archive.

Intel GPU memory
----------------
The local memory patch adds a GPU RAM (user) section with allocation and
residency bounds from readable i915 DRM clients of the calling user.
Repeated client IDs are deduplicated per PCI device. Shared buffer IDs are
not exported, so bounds replace a potentially double-counted point value.
An asterisk denotes incomplete visibility; unavailable data is N/A.
Definitions and limitations are in README.md.

Run the focused parser/accounting/procfs tests under ASan/UBSan:
  cmake -S . -B .deb-build/dev -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug \
    -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++-19 -DBTOP_LTO=OFF \
    '-DCMAKE_CXX_FLAGS=-fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer' \
    '-DCMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS=-fsanitize=address,undefined'
  cmake --build .deb-build/dev --target intel_gpu_memory_test -j 4
  .deb-build/dev/tests/intel_gpu_memory_test

Intel GPU access
----------------
The package sets CAP_PERFMON on /usr/bin/btop during configuration and
depends on libcap2-bin. The application runs as the calling user.
On Debian's kernel, perf_event_paranoid=3 blocks CAP_PERFMON as well.
Set it to 2 to permit the Intel PMU counters:
  sudo /usr/sbin/sysctl -w kernel.perf_event_paranoid=2

This also permits unprivileged profiling of a user's own userspace processes.
To retain the setting after reboot, an administrator can create
/etc/sysctl.d/90-btop-intel-gpu.conf containing:
  kernel.perf_event_paranoid = 2

The package does not change the system-wide sysctl setting automatically.

Install host tools:
  sudo apt install sbuild mmdebstrap uidmap devscripts debian-keyring debhelper lintian

Create the build environment as a regular user (once):
  mkdir -p ~/.cache/sbuild
  mmdebstrap --mode=unshare --variant=buildd --include=ca-certificates \
    --aptopt='APT::Update::Error-Mode "any"' --arch=amd64 --format=tar \
    --skip=output/mknod trixie ~/.cache/sbuild/trixie-amd64.tar \
    'deb https://deb.debian.org/debian trixie main' \
    'deb https://deb.debian.org/debian trixie-updates main' \
    'deb https://security.debian.org/debian-security trixie-security main'

Build from the repository root (the output directory is ignored by Git):
  mkdir -p .deb-build
  cd .deb-build
  dget --download-only https://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/main/b/btop/btop_1.4.7-1.dsc
  dpkg-source --before-build ..
  dpkg-source --build ..
  sbuild --chroot-mode=unshare --dist=trixie-backports --arch=amd64 --jobs=8 \
    --no-run-lintian --debbuildopt=-v1.3.2-0.1 \
    --chroot="$HOME/.cache/sbuild/trixie-amd64.tar" \
    btop_1.4.7-1~bpo13+1.dsc

Check the result with the host's lintian:
  lintian btop_1.4.7-1~bpo13+1.dsc \
    btop_1.4.7-1~bpo13+1_amd64.changes

Simulate installation with apt before installing the resulting amd64 .deb.

The working tree keeps the Debian patches applied for development.
